Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• `state_name`: to identify the State that the crash occurred in.  
• `day_of_week` / `day_of_week_name`: to keep only weekend crashes (1 = Sunday, 7 = Saturday).  
• `atmospheric_conditions_1_name` and `atmospheric_conditions_2_name` (or the already–combined `atmospheric_conditions_name`): to decide whether the crash happened during “Rain” (rain, rain & snow, heavy rain, etc.) or “Clear”.  
• `consecutive_number` (or any other column) is not needed for the aggregation, but it is a unique‑crash id if you need it for checking duplicates.

Typical WHERE‑clauses you will need  
```
-- weekend filter
WHERE day_of_week IN (1,7)

-- “rain” crashes (at least one of the two condition fields says Rain)
AND (
        LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) LIKE '%rain%' 
     OR LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_2_name) LIKE '%rain%'
    )

-- “clear” crashes – the usual FARS wording for clear weather is
-- 'No Adverse Condition (Clear)', but we do a fuzzy search on 'clear'
AND (
        LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_1_name) LIKE '%clear%' 
     OR LOWER(atmospheric_conditions_2_name) LIKE '%clear%'
    )
```

